1
00:00:17,608 --> 00:00:18,943
[WATSON gasps]

2
00:00:30,078 --> 00:00:31,789
This elaborate plan.

3
00:00:31,831 --> 00:00:34,416
Tell me, what was
their end goal?

4
00:00:36,084 --> 00:00:39,087
You're a man who
seeks out mysteries.

5
00:00:39,129 --> 00:00:42,925
There's no satisfaction
without a solution.

6
00:00:42,967 --> 00:00:48,263
And you want to know who created
the ultimate mystery, don't you?

7
00:00:48,305 --> 00:00:51,851
So shoot me and
you'll have your answer!

8
00:00:51,892 --> 00:00:53,769
[gun fires]

9
00:01:08,241 --> 00:01:10,160
[gasps]

10
00:01:17,751 --> 00:01:19,503
[grunts]

11
00:02:58,602 --> 00:03:01,229
[WATSON] Soon after solving
the murder of Lord Drebber.

12
00:03:01,271 --> 00:03:05,150
Sherlock's face was splashed
all over the newspapers.

13
00:03:05,191 --> 00:03:09,780
Almost overnight,
he came to prominence.

14
00:03:09,822 --> 00:03:12,157
Ever since,
we've had many visitors

15
00:03:12,198 --> 00:03:14,660
at two, two, one,
B Baker Street.

16
00:03:14,701 --> 00:03:18,956
Without fail, Sherlock solved
each case brought our way.

17
00:03:18,998 --> 00:03:22,208
One after another.

18
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:26,672
And yet, while his fame as
a detective continues to rise,

19
00:03:26,713 --> 00:03:30,258
within him, I sense
a storm brewing...

20
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:32,011
...more and more.

21
00:03:36,598 --> 00:03:40,019
[HOLMES grunts]
Such tedium.

22
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:41,061
What?

23
00:03:41,103 --> 00:03:43,480
I'm at a loss how things
ended up this way.

24
00:03:44,606 --> 00:03:46,066
With a mind like yours,

25
00:03:46,108 --> 00:03:48,902
you ought to be overjoyed at the
prospect of drowning in riddles.

26
00:03:48,944 --> 00:03:50,737
I've no idea what's
to complain about--

27
00:03:50,779 --> 00:03:52,156
Bah!

28
00:03:52,197 --> 00:03:55,784
I seek impenetrable mysteries,
John, not these banal chores!

29
00:03:55,826 --> 00:03:58,328
Oh? Like what?

30
00:03:58,370 --> 00:04:02,041
What else? The biggest
mystery I've yet to solve.

31
00:04:03,709 --> 00:04:06,712
[HOLMES] Who wanted my name
written in Drebber's blood

32
00:04:06,753 --> 00:04:09,506
daring me to crack the case?

33
00:04:09,548 --> 00:04:12,051
Hope called them his benefactor.

34
00:04:12,092 --> 00:04:16,013
The mastermind who
orchestrated the crime.

35
00:04:16,055 --> 00:04:20,726
I swear, I'll reveal who they
are and what their objective is.

36
00:04:20,767 --> 00:04:23,353
And, until then?

37
00:04:23,395 --> 00:04:27,607
[HOLMES grunts]
I wish to be alone for a while.

38
00:04:27,649 --> 00:04:29,151
Very well.

39
00:04:35,991 --> 00:04:38,451
[BARBER 10A] I've heard several
stories about your exploits,

40
00:04:38,493 --> 00:04:39,995
Inspector Lestrade.

41
00:04:40,037 --> 00:04:40,746
[LESTRADE coughs]

42
00:04:40,787 --> 00:04:41,788
Careful, sir!

43
00:04:41,830 --> 00:04:44,290
I don't want Scotland Yard
coming after me!

44
00:05:09,357 --> 00:05:11,484
[HOLMES]
During the case on the Noahtic,

45
00:05:11,526 --> 00:05:15,114
Enders was set up
by a mastermind.

46
00:05:15,155 --> 00:05:18,951
Later, Lord Drebber
is murdered by Hope

47
00:05:18,992 --> 00:05:22,287
under the direction of
some unknown individual.

48
00:05:24,081 --> 00:05:25,457
[HOLMES grunts]

49
00:05:51,233 --> 00:05:56,738
[HOPE] Sherlock Holmes.
I've heard much about you.

50
00:05:59,241 --> 00:06:03,703
[HOPE] I have a deal
which might intertest you.

51
00:06:03,745 --> 00:06:05,455
Pull the trigger.

52
00:06:05,497 --> 00:06:08,416
End my life and I'll
give you what you want.

53
00:06:08,458 --> 00:06:10,877
Answer to the ultimate mystery.

54
00:06:10,919 --> 00:06:13,964
The identity of the person
who was directing me.

55
00:06:14,006 --> 00:06:18,010
The one who plotted Drebber's
murder, start to finish.

56
00:06:18,051 --> 00:06:20,971
They hold you in high regard.

57
00:06:30,147 --> 00:06:31,690
And to such a degree,

58
00:06:31,731 --> 00:06:34,818
they wanted your name
left at the crime scene.

59
00:06:34,860 --> 00:06:37,654
This was a challenge to see
if you could solve the case,

60
00:06:37,696 --> 00:06:40,573
and thus prove you were
a worthy opponent.

61
00:06:42,159 --> 00:06:44,077
How can I describe 'em?

62
00:06:44,119 --> 00:06:47,622
You'll never meet another
with such an incredible mind.

63
00:06:49,374 --> 00:06:51,710
The moment I stepped foot
at Drebber's mansion

64
00:06:51,751 --> 00:06:53,086
at the appointed time,

65
00:06:53,128 --> 00:06:56,340
the door swung open
as if by some magic,

66
00:06:56,381 --> 00:06:59,176
and there was nary
a soul around to interfere.

67
00:06:59,218 --> 00:07:02,721
My benefactor truly
is a criminal genius!

68
00:07:02,762 --> 00:07:05,682
[HOLMES] And someone
like that wishes to test me?

69
00:07:05,724 --> 00:07:08,852
But why? To what end?!

70
00:07:13,190 --> 00:07:14,858
[grunts]

71
00:07:21,447 --> 00:07:24,826
[HOLMES]
What if, at this very moment...

72
00:07:24,868 --> 00:07:26,578
[HOLMES groans]

73
00:07:38,715 --> 00:07:41,760
[laughing]

74
00:07:41,801 --> 00:07:43,136
[HOLMES]
Meanwhile,

75
00:07:43,178 --> 00:07:46,598
they're still out there
somewhere, free to kill.

76
00:07:46,639 --> 00:07:49,642
[laughing]

77
00:07:51,686 --> 00:07:53,355
- -[gun fires]
- -[gasps]

78
00:07:53,397 --> 00:07:54,689
- -[gun fires]
- -[gasps]

79
00:07:54,731 --> 00:07:57,276
[gun firing]

80
00:07:59,778 --> 00:08:01,780
[HUDSON panting]

81
00:08:03,949 --> 00:08:05,409
John! What's going on?

82
00:08:05,450 --> 00:08:07,244
I'm not entirely sure!

83
00:08:07,286 --> 00:08:09,704
But I definitely know
where it came from!

84
00:08:09,746 --> 00:08:11,206
Sherlock! What--?

85
00:08:11,248 --> 00:08:12,457
[BOTH gasp]

86
00:08:17,963 --> 00:08:20,966
It appears we've
discovered the triggerman.

87
00:08:24,552 --> 00:08:26,596
[LESTRADE] I heard shots!
Are you all right?

88
00:08:26,638 --> 00:08:29,433
Well, we are, but Sherlock...

89
00:08:31,810 --> 00:08:34,646
I see. So the shots
were from you?

90
00:08:34,687 --> 00:08:37,023
[HOLMES]
Yeah, to get you to come up.

91
00:08:37,065 --> 00:08:38,108
[grunts]

92
00:08:38,150 --> 00:08:40,152
[HOLMES] You're far too
predictable.

93
00:08:40,193 --> 00:08:41,694
At this hour, you're off-duty

94
00:08:41,736 --> 00:08:44,823
getting a routine trim
at the nearby barber.

95
00:08:44,864 --> 00:08:47,617
[gasps] Then come get me
like a normal person!

96
00:08:47,659 --> 00:08:49,369
[HOLMES]
Lestrade.

97
00:08:49,411 --> 00:08:51,579
Have there been any
recent cases involving

98
00:08:51,621 --> 00:08:54,416
the sudden deaths
of aristocrats?

99
00:08:54,458 --> 00:08:57,794
That's rather specific.
Why are you inquiring?

100
00:08:57,836 --> 00:09:00,505
[HOLMES] Just tell me.
Have there been any?

101
00:09:00,546 --> 00:09:02,257
[LESTRADE]
Hmm.

102
00:09:03,175 --> 00:09:06,052
[HOLMES] Now that I no longer
have a line on this fellow,

103
00:09:06,094 --> 00:09:09,139
I have no choice but to
grasp at any possibility

104
00:09:09,181 --> 00:09:10,515
no matter how slim.

105
00:09:10,556 --> 00:09:14,102
[LESTRADE] Well actually, I
heard the Viscount of Redshire

106
00:09:14,144 --> 00:09:16,188
passed away on
his estate in York.

107
00:09:16,229 --> 00:09:17,022
Tell me more!

108
00:09:17,063 --> 00:09:19,065
[GROUP gasps]

109
00:09:19,107 --> 00:09:21,026
[LESTRADE]
Let's see. As I recall,

110
00:09:21,067 --> 00:09:24,612
he wrote a note saying he was
going for late night walk,

111
00:09:24,654 --> 00:09:27,573
then collapsed near
the lakefront.

112
00:09:27,615 --> 00:09:30,494
The man was ill and there
was no sign of foul play,

113
00:09:30,535 --> 00:09:33,246
so the death was ruled natural.

114
00:09:33,288 --> 00:09:36,708
Now that I've told you,
what are your intentions?

115
00:09:36,749 --> 00:09:38,502
We're taking a trip to York.

116
00:09:38,543 --> 00:09:40,670
And why would we do that?

117
00:09:40,712 --> 00:09:41,838
Isn't it obvious?

118
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:44,508
To see whether or not
his death was a homicide.

119
00:09:44,549 --> 00:09:46,801
But I just said--!

120
00:10:40,188 --> 00:10:41,940
[LESTRADE]
For heaven's sake.

121
00:10:41,981 --> 00:10:44,025
I was more than willing
to humour you and

122
00:10:44,067 --> 00:10:46,570
re-examine Lord
Redshire's death,

123
00:10:46,611 --> 00:10:49,655
but only because I thought
you'd uncovered some new facts.

124
00:10:49,697 --> 00:10:50,823
We're here.

125
00:10:57,872 --> 00:11:00,917
After the viscount left his
mansion for an evening walk,

126
00:11:00,959 --> 00:11:03,669
where exactly did the
body ultimately end up?

127
00:11:03,711 --> 00:11:05,422
Should be... Yeah.

128
00:11:05,464 --> 00:11:06,798
That's right.

129
00:11:06,839 --> 00:11:09,551
It was right beside
the base of that oak tree.

130
00:11:15,181 --> 00:11:16,933
Several days have passed.

131
00:11:16,975 --> 00:11:18,310
If this was a crime,

132
00:11:18,351 --> 00:11:21,187
I rather suspect there aren't
any clues left to be found.

133
00:11:21,229 --> 00:11:23,940
[HOLMES] Mind your own
business and keep quiet!

134
00:11:31,156 --> 00:11:33,032
[RAILWAYMAN 10A]
Passengers for London!

135
00:11:33,074 --> 00:11:36,244
Your train will be departing
from platform three!

136
00:11:36,286 --> 00:11:40,665
If you haven't boarded at this
time, please do so immediately!

137
00:11:40,706 --> 00:11:43,751
Damn it. I came all
the way to York for nothing.

138
00:11:43,793 --> 00:11:45,920
The old man croaked
from illness.

139
00:11:45,962 --> 00:11:47,506
What a waste of time.

140
00:11:47,547 --> 00:11:49,882
[laughs] What'd I say?

141
00:11:49,924 --> 00:11:51,635
Looks like you bet
on the wrong horse.

142
00:11:51,676 --> 00:11:53,011
[LESTRADE gasps]

143
00:11:53,970 --> 00:11:57,849
Ahem! Think I'll go buy
a paper for the ride home.

144
00:11:57,890 --> 00:12:00,352
I'll find you later
and catch a drink.

145
00:12:00,393 --> 00:12:03,771
Well then, see you aboard,
Inspector Lestrade!

146
00:12:03,813 --> 00:12:06,274
God. Why couldn't
you die properly?

147
00:12:06,316 --> 00:12:07,859
To hell with death by illness.

148
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:10,445
Holmes! Really!
You're acting like a child.

149
00:12:10,487 --> 00:12:12,989
Just because things didn't
end up going your way--

150
00:12:13,031 --> 00:12:14,907
This is unbecoming of you.

151
00:12:14,949 --> 00:12:17,035
Damnnit, I'm outta lights.

152
00:12:17,076 --> 00:12:18,828
John, gimme a match.

153
00:12:18,870 --> 00:12:20,413
No, I think not!

154
00:12:20,455 --> 00:12:22,874
And you know that this excess
is bad for your health!

155
00:12:22,915 --> 00:12:24,501
You should quit!

156
00:12:24,543 --> 00:12:25,502
Would you shut up

157
00:12:25,544 --> 00:12:27,837
and just give me
a blasted match already?

158
00:12:27,879 --> 00:12:31,508
Why must the most brilliant man
I've ever met lack such decency?

159
00:12:31,550 --> 00:12:35,178
Ugh. This useless doctor
can't even light a cigarette!

160
00:12:35,220 --> 00:12:36,680
What is that supposed to mean?

161
00:12:36,721 --> 00:12:39,391
This entire mystery
would've been solved by now

162
00:12:39,432 --> 00:12:41,643
if you hadn't gone
and interfered!

163
00:12:41,685 --> 00:12:43,478
I beg your pardon?

164
00:12:43,520 --> 00:12:46,272
You heard! Why'd you
have to stop me?

165
00:12:46,314 --> 00:12:48,149
If I'm not mistaken,

166
00:12:48,191 --> 00:12:50,402
you mean when I prevented you
from killing Hope.

167
00:12:50,443 --> 00:12:51,736
Yeah, exactly!

168
00:12:51,777 --> 00:12:54,113
If I wasn't there,
are you really saying

169
00:12:54,155 --> 00:12:56,616
that you would've shot
the man right where he stood?

170
00:12:56,658 --> 00:12:58,868
[HOLMES growls]

171
00:12:58,910 --> 00:13:02,997
And why not? At least then
I wouldn't suffer this enigma.

172
00:13:03,039 --> 00:13:04,790
You don't mean that! [grunts]

173
00:13:04,832 --> 00:13:07,335
Hope and I would've
both got what we wanted!

174
00:13:07,377 --> 00:13:08,878
A quick death for him

175
00:13:08,920 --> 00:13:11,964
and for me, the answer
to this damn mystery!

176
00:13:13,174 --> 00:13:16,511
I'm speechless.
You're no sleuth.

177
00:13:16,553 --> 00:13:19,598
All along you were nothing
but a worthless scoundrel.

178
00:13:19,639 --> 00:13:20,806
What?

179
00:13:20,848 --> 00:13:22,100
[WATSON grunts]

180
00:13:22,141 --> 00:13:24,852
If you wish to be a murderer,
then do so on your own.

181
00:13:24,894 --> 00:13:28,607
I refuse to be an accomplice
in your degeneracy.

182
00:13:28,648 --> 00:13:30,316
Good day.

183
00:13:35,739 --> 00:13:37,741
[HOLMES]
How dramatic.

184
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:41,620
[RAILWAYMAN 10A] The London
train is departing shortly!

185
00:13:41,661 --> 00:13:44,539
Final call for passengers!

186
00:13:45,498 --> 00:13:47,250
[grunts]

187
00:14:08,187 --> 00:14:09,689
[HOLMES]
It's locked?

188
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:20,199
[HOLMES grumbles]

189
00:14:20,241 --> 00:14:22,910
Fine, then. Food it is.

190
00:14:31,419 --> 00:14:33,588
[WAITER 10A]
Will this table do, sir?

191
00:14:33,630 --> 00:14:35,089
Yeah.

192
00:14:36,424 --> 00:14:37,759
Confound him.

193
00:14:37,801 --> 00:14:40,136
He's like an old maid.

194
00:14:40,178 --> 00:14:42,221
Lecturing me.

195
00:14:44,849 --> 00:14:47,977
[HOLMES]
Hm. Hmm?

196
00:14:48,019 --> 00:14:49,270
I'm switching tables!

197
00:14:49,312 --> 00:14:51,856
[WAITER 10A grunts]
Where to, sir?

198
00:14:57,529 --> 00:14:59,030
Well, now.

199
00:14:59,071 --> 00:15:00,782
Professor.

200
00:15:02,450 --> 00:15:04,536
What are the odds?

201
00:15:15,254 --> 00:15:16,881
Well, now.

202
00:15:16,922 --> 00:15:19,133
Professor.

203
00:15:19,175 --> 00:15:21,678
What are the odds?

204
00:15:21,720 --> 00:15:23,763
[LOUIS]
Sherlock Holmes!

205
00:15:25,515 --> 00:15:27,475
[LOUIS]
What's he doing here?

206
00:15:28,934 --> 00:15:30,228
I remember.

207
00:15:30,269 --> 00:15:32,897
You and I met while we
were aboard the Noahtic.

208
00:15:32,938 --> 00:15:34,232
The name's Sherlock.

209
00:15:34,273 --> 00:15:36,275
You're quite
famous these days.

210
00:15:36,317 --> 00:15:38,528
I recently read in the
newspapers how you solved

211
00:15:38,570 --> 00:15:40,864
the case of
Lord Drebber's murder.

212
00:15:40,905 --> 00:15:42,948
That is the real mystery.

213
00:15:42,990 --> 00:15:46,619
My intention was for Lestrade
to take all the credit for it.

214
00:15:46,661 --> 00:15:49,330
Either a reporter
sniffed out the truth,

215
00:15:49,372 --> 00:15:51,374
or someone leaked it.

216
00:15:51,416 --> 00:15:53,084
And that's a problem?

217
00:15:53,125 --> 00:15:56,128
As a result, your fame
as a detective has grown.

218
00:15:56,170 --> 00:15:57,672
Isn't that good for business?

219
00:15:57,714 --> 00:16:00,174
[HOLMES]
Hmm. It pays, yes.

220
00:16:00,216 --> 00:16:03,010
But between us, I'm not much
interested in all those dull,

221
00:16:03,052 --> 00:16:04,512
tuppenny-ha'penny cases.

222
00:16:04,554 --> 00:16:06,055
What fascinates me is--

223
00:16:06,097 --> 00:16:09,434
Pardon me, but we were
finishing our meal.

224
00:16:09,475 --> 00:16:10,894
Perhaps sometime later, you--

225
00:16:10,935 --> 00:16:14,647
But you haven't even been served
your dessert or digestif yet.

226
00:16:14,689 --> 00:16:15,815
[LOUIS]
This pest.

227
00:16:15,857 --> 00:16:18,526
[HOLMES] Permit me to bend
your ear for a minute.

228
00:16:18,568 --> 00:16:20,278
By all means, please.

229
00:16:20,319 --> 00:16:23,489
We were just complaining about
how we were growing bored.

230
00:16:23,531 --> 00:16:26,951
[HOLMES] You see, I went to
York to investigate a case

231
00:16:26,992 --> 00:16:30,162
only for it to end up
being a total bust.

232
00:16:30,204 --> 00:16:32,290
Well, that certainly is a pity.

233
00:16:32,331 --> 00:16:34,292
A damn shame. And get this--

234
00:16:34,333 --> 00:16:37,211
I was convinced it was
another in a series of deaths

235
00:16:37,253 --> 00:16:39,046
involving noblemen.

236
00:16:39,088 --> 00:16:41,758
There's a series of them?

237
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:44,886
Yes. Two so far.

238
00:16:44,928 --> 00:16:48,890
And both have commonalities that
I believe link them together.

239
00:16:48,932 --> 00:16:52,059
The victims, Enders and
Drebber, were aristocrats.

240
00:16:52,101 --> 00:16:54,813
Additionally, their
scandals and misdeeds

241
00:16:54,854 --> 00:16:56,606
were brought to
the public's attention.

242
00:16:56,648 --> 00:16:59,275
And in glorious fashion,
I might add.

243
00:16:59,317 --> 00:17:01,736
But there's one
other trait they share.

244
00:17:01,778 --> 00:17:03,195
In both cases,

245
00:17:03,237 --> 00:17:06,741
a mastermind was operating
behind the scenes.

246
00:17:06,783 --> 00:17:07,951
To the first point,

247
00:17:07,992 --> 00:17:11,245
it doesn't strike me as
being particularly solid.

248
00:17:11,287 --> 00:17:15,249
Two similar events reveal a
coincidence, not a pattern.

249
00:17:15,291 --> 00:17:18,586
Second, the existence
of this mastermind--

250
00:17:18,628 --> 00:17:21,380
isn't it merely
your own presumption?

251
00:17:22,507 --> 00:17:24,926
Well, you'd be
correct, except

252
00:17:24,968 --> 00:17:27,971
my suspicions were
recently confirmed.

253
00:17:28,012 --> 00:17:30,264
This individual
made me a proposal,

254
00:17:30,306 --> 00:17:32,725
extended via Jefferson Hope.

255
00:17:32,767 --> 00:17:34,769
A most interesting one.

256
00:17:34,811 --> 00:17:36,855
If I ended Hope's life,

257
00:17:36,896 --> 00:17:39,357
they would reveal
their identity.

258
00:17:39,398 --> 00:17:41,693
[WILLIAM]
That is quite the odd bargain.

259
00:17:41,734 --> 00:17:43,277
[HOLMES]
Isn't it?

260
00:17:43,319 --> 00:17:47,114
But I didn't accept the terms
and refused to shoot Hope.

261
00:17:47,156 --> 00:17:49,659
[WILLIAM]
A wise decision.

262
00:17:49,701 --> 00:17:53,245
[HOLMES] However, after
he was taken into custody,

263
00:17:53,287 --> 00:17:57,249
I went to visit him,
where he told me a secret.

264
00:17:57,291 --> 00:18:00,753
He felt compelled to reveal
to me who his benefactor was.

265
00:18:00,795 --> 00:18:02,964
The name of the mastermind.

266
00:18:08,011 --> 00:18:09,303
To my shock,

267
00:18:09,345 --> 00:18:13,850
the person he named
is sitting here.

268
00:18:13,892 --> 00:18:16,895
William James Moriarty.

269
00:18:24,318 --> 00:18:27,864
[LOUIS]
Is that the truth or a bluff?

270
00:18:27,906 --> 00:18:30,449
Finding out about
Brother would be simple

271
00:18:30,491 --> 00:18:34,495
if he had sought out
information aboard the Noahtic.

272
00:18:34,537 --> 00:18:35,788
After all,

273
00:18:35,830 --> 00:18:38,875
there aren't many young college
mathematics professors

274
00:18:38,917 --> 00:18:41,252
who are also
sons of aristocrats.

275
00:18:42,962 --> 00:18:44,839
[LOUIS]
Also, after his arrest,

276
00:18:44,881 --> 00:18:47,466
we've been keeping
a close eye on Hope

277
00:18:47,508 --> 00:18:50,219
and would have seen if
he had contact with anyone.

278
00:18:51,136 --> 00:18:54,557
[LOUIS]
Meaning, this must be a ruse.

279
00:18:54,599 --> 00:18:56,684
Brother's name
is still a secret.

280
00:18:56,726 --> 00:18:59,062
Holmes doesn't know.

281
00:18:59,103 --> 00:19:01,397
There's just one problem.

282
00:19:01,439 --> 00:19:03,733
If William calls his bluff,

283
00:19:03,775 --> 00:19:07,361
he'll acknowledge we were
monitoring Hope closely.

284
00:19:07,403 --> 00:19:10,239
Damn it. Holmes, you bastard.

285
00:19:10,281 --> 00:19:13,200
You need to be removed
before it's too late.

286
00:19:15,745 --> 00:19:17,789
I confess...

287
00:19:17,830 --> 00:19:20,416
...that you've made
a most amusing assertion.

288
00:19:20,458 --> 00:19:25,088
However, it is impossible to
prove something I did not do.

289
00:19:25,129 --> 00:19:27,006
And besides,

290
00:19:27,048 --> 00:19:29,759
the burden of proof would
be your responsibility

291
00:19:29,801 --> 00:19:32,261
seeing as you're the detective.

292
00:19:34,221 --> 00:19:37,308
[HOLMES laughing]

293
00:19:37,349 --> 00:19:39,435
A joke! I'm kidding!

294
00:19:39,477 --> 00:19:42,814
There's a case of probatio
diabolica. Too good!

295
00:19:42,855 --> 00:19:45,441
Really! [chuckles]

296
00:19:45,483 --> 00:19:49,570
However, if you were
indeed this mastermind,

297
00:19:49,612 --> 00:19:52,531
just how would you
reply in this instance?

298
00:19:55,159 --> 00:19:56,577
[WILLIAM chuckles]

299
00:19:58,871 --> 00:20:00,123
[HOLMES]
Hm?

300
00:20:00,164 --> 00:20:02,708
"Come and catch me if you can...

301
00:20:02,750 --> 00:20:04,585
...Mister Holmes."

302
00:20:07,296 --> 00:20:09,674
Hmm!

303
00:20:09,715 --> 00:20:12,384
Yes, I suspect you
would derive satisfaction

304
00:20:12,426 --> 00:20:16,263
at hearing those words,
detective.

305
00:20:16,305 --> 00:20:19,475
[laughs]

306
00:20:19,517 --> 00:20:21,477
[HOLMES]
You really are one of a kind.

307
00:20:21,519 --> 00:20:22,728
Judging by your response,

308
00:20:22,770 --> 00:20:25,106
it sounds like my joke
was well received.

309
00:20:25,148 --> 00:20:26,149
Yeah!

310
00:20:26,190 --> 00:20:28,734
You and I are going
to get along famously!

311
00:20:28,776 --> 00:20:29,694
[LESTRADE]
Holmes.

312
00:20:29,735 --> 00:20:32,488
Ah? So there you are.

313
00:20:32,530 --> 00:20:35,324
Well, tell me, who might
these gentlemen be?

314
00:20:35,366 --> 00:20:38,327
This is the Moriarty family's
second son, William,

315
00:20:38,369 --> 00:20:40,788
and his adoptive
younger brother, Louis.

316
00:20:40,830 --> 00:20:41,873
Is that right?

317
00:20:41,914 --> 00:20:43,082
[LOUIS]
Mm.

318
00:20:43,124 --> 00:20:45,334
[LOUIS] Our family...
Just how much does he know?

319
00:20:45,376 --> 00:20:47,378
Please, excuse the interruption.

320
00:20:47,419 --> 00:20:50,048
I am Detective Inspector
Lestrade of Scotland Yard,

321
00:20:50,089 --> 00:20:51,340
at your service.

322
00:20:51,382 --> 00:20:53,676
[WILLIAM] The Lestrade?
It's an honor, sir.

323
00:20:53,718 --> 00:20:54,468
[WOMAN 10A screams]

324
00:20:54,510 --> 00:20:55,761
[GROUP gasps]

325
00:20:59,682 --> 00:21:01,726
[WOMAN 10A gasps]

326
00:21:01,767 --> 00:21:03,102
[LESTRADE]
What's wrong?

327
00:21:03,144 --> 00:21:05,521
Well, I heard a loud
sound in the neighboring

328
00:21:05,563 --> 00:21:07,565
compartment just moments ago.

329
00:21:07,606 --> 00:21:10,275
And when I looked through
the window I saw...

330
00:21:11,402 --> 00:21:12,653
[LESTRADE gasps]

331
00:21:16,490 --> 00:21:18,784
[LESTRADE grunts]
Door's locked!

332
00:21:18,826 --> 00:21:21,162
Holmes, this can't be good.

333
00:21:21,204 --> 00:21:23,539
Yeah. Very dead.

334
00:21:23,581 --> 00:21:25,166
Have a look.

335
00:21:26,458 --> 00:21:28,169
It was quite clearly a murder.

336
00:21:28,211 --> 00:21:29,670
Murder?

337
00:21:32,506 --> 00:21:34,383
So, William,

338
00:21:34,425 --> 00:21:37,261
if you don't have any pressing
matters before we reach London,

339
00:21:37,302 --> 00:21:38,679
how about a friendly competition

340
00:21:38,721 --> 00:21:40,723
to see which of us
finds the culprit first?

341
00:21:40,765 --> 00:21:43,101
[LESTRADE]
Huh? You're not serious!

342
00:21:43,142 --> 00:21:44,810
This is a murder,
not one of your games!

343
00:21:44,852 --> 00:21:48,689
I accept. No doubt
it'll prove entertaining.

344
00:21:48,731 --> 00:21:49,982
However,

345
00:21:50,024 --> 00:21:52,526
this case seems more complicated
than at first glance.

346
00:21:52,568 --> 00:21:54,237
[GROUP]
Huh?

347
00:23:34,920 --> 00:23:37,548
[HOLMES] Next time
on Moriarty the Patriot  .

348
00:23:37,589 --> 00:23:40,176
"The Two Detectives. Act Two."
